{
  "comment": "Shared contract for the natural-language time parser. The canonical implementation is clients/core/src/time.ts; backend/public/nl-time.js is a hand port for the no-build dashboard. Both the TypeScript suite and the browser-port suite assert these cases, so the two implementations cannot drift. `now` is a LOCAL wall-clock ISO date-time written WITHOUT a timezone offset: ECMAScript parses offset-less date-time forms as local time, which keeps the fixture timezone-portable (the parser is defined entirely in local wall-clock terms). expected fields are read with getFullYear/getMonth+1/getDate/getHours/getMinutes/getSeconds.",
  "rule": {
    "grammar": [
      "duration: <n>m / <n>min, <n>h, <n>h<n>m, <n>s / <n>seconds, optionally led by the 'in ' connector; never combined with a day qualifier",
      "clock: bare hour (24-hour), H:MM (24-hour without meridiem), Ham/pm, H:MMam/pm; 'at' and 'in' connectors are stripped",
      "day qualifier: today, tomorrow | tmr, a weekday name",
      "recurrence: daily, weekdays, weekends, 'every day', 'every <weekday list/range>', a bare weekday list/range",
      "list/range: mon-fri (inclusive wrapping), mon,wed,fri, and spaced 'mon, wed, fri'"
    ],
    "fields": {
      "name": "human label for the case",
      "input": "the expression handed to parseTime",
      "now": "local wall-clock ISO date-time (no offset) injected as `now`",
      "expect": "local wall-clock fields of the resulting targetTime",
      "days": "repeatDays as ISO weekdays, 1=Mon..7=Sun; [] means one-time"
    },
    "notes": [
      "Grammar is matched as the longest leading prefix of a single expression, never a concatenation.",
      "parseLeadingTime prefers the longest leading prefix; when none parses it falls back to the longest parseable span anywhere in the input (trailing spans preferred) and returns the surrounding text as the label, so label-first phrasing like 'gym tomorrow 7am' works.",
      "A bare or colon time without a meridiem is read as 24-hour; a meridiem implies a 1-12 hour (13pm and 0am are invalid).",
      "Passed times roll forward; a passed 'today' rolls to tomorrow; a weekday that has passed rolls to next week.",
      "The interpretation echo is formatLocal + formatRelative, e.g. 'Tue 22 Sep, 7:00am (in 13h 40m)'."
    ]
  },
